# Caldera Sapper — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Sapper, the network-cutting technician of the
Slag — a tall, spindly, insectile obsidian creature that stalks on four thin high-kneed
legs, holds its narrow body high on a long segmented spine, and cuts pipes and structures
with two long shear arms — as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime.
Build it with `voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged
model that reads unmistakably as this spindly obsidian thing, stalks believably on its
legs, and cuts on demand. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes
what the Sapper is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts
list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a stalking, cutting
creature needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Sapper, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Sapper faces toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front) at rest, the shear arms
  reaching forward. Up is +y.
- Build the Sapper symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane — the four legs
  and the two shear arms mirror left/right, and the body, spine, and head are centered
  on it.
- The Sapper is deliberately **tall and spindly** — a narrow body carried high on thin
  legs, its segmented spine rising most of the height of the volume to a small head. It
  is a precise technician; nothing about it should read as armored, heavy, low, or
  massive.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same
  volume's coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Sapper (a leg
  already under the high body, a shear arm already reaching forward).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Body — obsidian | `#241f2b` |
| Deep recess, shadowed seam — obsidian dark | `#16131b` |
| Accent plate (the accent region) | `#4a4358` |
| Acid glow — the spine beads | `#9ede3a` |

Do **not** use steel `#c9ced6` or violet `#b56bff` anywhere on the model. A game
paints those on at run time (see *The accent region*); baking them in defeats it.

## What the Sapper is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Sapper must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **tall, narrow, insectile body** of obsidian, held high off the ground and carried
  on a **long segmented spine** that curves up to a small, narrow head. Its shadowed
  seams and recesses are obsidian dark.
- **Four thin, high-kneed legs**, one at each corner, that carry it and stalk (see the
  animations). They read as thin, jointed, and spidery — the legs of a light stepper,
  not a heavy walker.
- **Two long multi-jointed cutting shear arms** reaching from the front — scissoring
  blades it uses to cut pipes and structures. These are **tools**, not fists: it is a
  technician, not a brawler, and nothing about it reads as armored or heavy.
- **A row of acid-green beads running along each segment of the spine**, and no other
  glow anywhere on the model. There is **no eye and no chest seams**: that beaded spine
  is the Sapper's whole glow signature.

The Sapper carries **no armor plating** and no weapon but its shears — its shear arms
are its whole armament, and it is the only Slag that carries tools. It is the tall, thin
one: nothing about it reads as low, long, fast, wide, top-heavy, or massive.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the body,
spine, and head, the number of segments and joints in each leg and each shear arm, how
the beads run along the spine, and how you break the Sapper into rig parts. Nothing here
prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the
Sapper and animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the Sapper's **tier**, by finding every
voxel of one reserved color and repainting it. That reserved color is the **accent
plate** color `#4a4358`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **plates along the outer faces of both cutting shear arms** — and only
  those — in `#4a4358`.
- Use `#4a4358` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not
  a seam. Every voxel of it is treated as an accent plate.
- The accent must be **coherent contiguous plates** — one plate group per shear arm,
  not scattered flecks — and it must be clearly **visible from more than one angle**, so
  a player reads a Sapper's tier at a glance across the battlefield.

Everything else — the body, the recesses, the legs, the spine, the head, the spine
beads — keeps its own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and every Sapper in
the game looks the same tier.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author
the motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`,
choosing the period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion has the right character — the Sapper is light and precise, so its motion is quick
and spidery, not the heavy ease of a big machine.

- **`move`** — the stalk (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The four thin legs
  pick their way with a quick, spidery, high-stepping gait while the narrow body stays
  held high and level, so it reads as a light, precise creature stepping, not gliding or
  flailing. Authored in place: the body does not translate across the scene — the leg
  cycle alone carries the stride (played on its own the planted foot slides straight back
  under the body, treadmill-style, then swings forward), and a game supplies the real
  travel. The legs work; the body holds high and level.
- **`attack`** — the cut (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The two shear arms
  open wide and scissor shut in one clean cutting stroke, the blades meeting at the
  close, then reset to their stance before looping. It reads as cutting through a pipe or
  structure, not punching. The feet stay planted.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a
slight sway of the high body at rest), but you must not drop or contradict the required
`move` and `attack` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a
pivot), sculpt each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`,
place joints with `define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and
a mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation;
run `voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png`
previews) between calls to judge each part against this brief — that each leg seats under
the high body, the segmented spine curves up unbroken to the small head, the two shear
arms reach out from the front, and the animations read with the right lightness. Run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result
is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>`
renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model
posed at that instant to check the motion).
